<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Xizhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">303–361</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Xizhi was a Chinese calligrapher of the Eastern Jin dynasty, revered as the 'Sage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">Calligraphy</a>'. A member of the powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-clan-of-langya">Wang clan of Langya</a>, he served as an official in Kuaiji. His preface to the poems composed at the Orchid Pavilion gathering of 353 became the most celebrated work of Chinese calligraphy; no originals survive, but copies and rubbings set the standard for running and regular script for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wang-xizhi.jpg" alt="Wang Xizhi" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Chinese artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Wang_Xizhi_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
